{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["standardization","feature scaling","accuracy"],"description":"The evidence primarily focuses on Z-score standardization as the superior method for improving KNN accuracy, but there is limited comparative analysis of other standardization techniques (e.g., robust scaling, min-max normalization) across diverse datasets and feature distributions. While E1 states that Z-score standardization outperforms Min–Max normalization, the underlying reasons—such as sensitivity to outliers or distributional assumptions—are not explored in depth.","evidence_ids":["E1"],"id":"G1","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"36a8aed53297df89b948628e45613490555a98694fcf073ddc8ae1a1ba5653ab","kind":"artefact","prev":"388c5ec0b05926f796f38c9a120787c586c6ad48f674bac5c4c6502690092a3c","seq":24,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.675+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["tabular data","feature scaling","accuracy"],"description":"Most studies evaluate KNN performance in specific domains (e.g., heart disease, diabetes, student outcomes), with only a few generalizing to tabular data with varying scales. For instance, E5 and E10 apply standardization in diabetes and student outcome prediction, but these are narrow domains and may not represent generalizable patterns across diverse tabular datasets.","evidence_ids":["E5","E10"],"id":"G2","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"1e5c4f2b60507202cd13e835572be8c481d4d7a1544873f6ec773159d7c64c3d","kind":"artefact","prev":"36a8aed53297df89b948628e45613490555a98694fcf073ddc8ae1a1ba5653ab","seq":25,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.675+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["distance metric","hyperparameter tuning","feature scaling"],"description":"The impact of feature scaling on KNN accuracy is acknowledged, but the influence of scaling on different distance metrics (e.g., Euclidean vs. Manhattan) or the interplay with hyperparameter tuning (e.g., k-value, weight function) remains underexplored. While E2 mentions parameter optimization improving accuracy, it does not isolate the contribution of scaling from other tuning effects.","evidence_ids":["E2"],"id":"G3","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"bba22bde4acbea532751997b607ca4f46139fc52874ad12881a21ebaab582714","kind":"artefact","prev":"1e5c4f2b60507202cd13e835572be8c481d4d7a1544873f6ec773159d7c64c3d","seq":26,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.676+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["standardization","alternative methods","robustness","efficiency"],"description":"No evidence directly compares the effectiveness of standardization versus alternative preprocessing methods (e.g., robust scaling, data transformation) in terms of computational efficiency, robustness to outliers, or generalization across data types, leaving open questions about practical trade-offs in real-world applications.","evidence_ids":["E1","E4","E5"],"id":"G4","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"edd48fc10393ae74fc204d1f91aeff37fdb81e43bdac719555557aebef00cfea","kind":"artefact","prev":"bba22bde4acbea532751997b607ca4f46139fc52874ad12881a21ebaab582714","seq":27,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.676+00:00"}
{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"hypothesis"},"hash":"de0ad37f2df94500c7b9628f1a54aaf7f52e74b4d213ff5e86194719573b0c15","kind":"stage","prev":"edd48fc10393ae74fc204d1f91aeff37fdb81e43bdac719555557aebef00cfea","seq":28,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.676+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"KNN classification accuracy","evidence_ids":["E1"],"expected_direction":"increase","gap_ids":["G1"],"id":"H1","independent_variable":"type of feature scaling (Z-score vs. min-max normalization)","rationale":"E1 provides empirical evidence that Z-score standardization yields higher and more stable accuracy than min-max normalization. This hypothesis directly addresses the gap in comparative analysis across diverse distributions and feature scales, testing a specific claim about the superiority of Z-score standardization in non-normal or mixed-scaled data.","statement":"Feature scaling using Z-score standardization improves KNN classification accuracy more than min-max normalization in tabular datasets with mixed feature scales and non-normal distributions."},"hash":"ae1360ac8d1ee18430cf268c604b96529804b3ebf727dd4fe9771119e323e3bd","kind":"artefact","prev":"de0ad37f2df94500c7b9628f1a54aaf7f52e74b4d213ff5e86194719573b0c15","seq":29,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.676+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"KNN classification accuracy","evidence_ids":["E2"],"expected_direction":"no_difference","gap_ids":["G3"],"id":"H2","independent_variable":"distance metric used (Euclidean vs. Manhattan)","rationale":"E2 shows that parameter optimization (including metric selection) improves accuracy, but does not isolate the effect of scaling from distance metric choice. This hypothesis tests whether scaling's benefit is independent of the distance metric, addressing the gap in understanding the interplay between scaling and distance metrics.","statement":"The performance gain from feature scaling in KNN is consistent across different distance metrics (Euclidean vs. Manhattan) in tabular datasets with varying feature scales."},"hash":"cca64ad2444a35be15c9f57b5db27cf15edb66670fc5ce52d99ed5df2cb7c449","kind":"artefact","prev":"ae1360ac8d1ee18430cf268c604b96529804b3ebf727dd4fe9771119e323e3bd","seq":30,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:10.676+00:00"}
