{"actor":"experiment","data":{"evidence_ids":["E2"],"hypothesis_id":"H2","id":"K1","limitations":["The experiment design does not include a variation of distance metrics (Euclidean vs. Manhattan), which is essential to test the hypothesis as written.","No dataset or treatment condition in the evidence includes a direct comparison of Euclidean and Manhattan distance metrics under feature scaling.","The comparisons in the evidence only report overall accuracy gains from scaling (e.g., 97.19% vs. 93.86%), without isolating the effect of distance metric choice.","The Holm-adjusted p-values for scaling effects (e.g., p_holm = 0.008656 in breast_cancer) indicate significance, but these do not address the interaction between scaling and distance metric."],"result_id":"R2","statement":"The hypothesis cannot be evaluated as stated because the design does not include variation in distance metrics (Euclidean vs. Manhattan) as a factor. The provided evidence (E2) shows that a Manhattan-based KNN with scaling achieved 97.19% accuracy, but no comparison is made between Euclidean and Manhattan under feature scaling. Without data on accuracy differences between Euclidean and Manhattan distance metrics with and without scaling, the claim of a consistent performance gain from scaling across metrics cannot be tested or supported.","verdict":"inconclusive"},"hash":"a68879ac45b8078657723c5454afbe91e44c42784f08f7d37c00071b289de19b","kind":"artefact","prev":"fd5ff7aba0d013742fce55f8a3c08e041f9694a7f033c5cdeac48219cc3c9aac","seq":51,"stage":"conclusion","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:13.162+00:00"}
